          %h3#references_3 References
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gianfranco+Mascari"}> J-F Mascari
              , D Giacchero and N Sfakianakis,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Symetries and asymetries of the immune system response: A categorification approach
              %a{:href => "http://www.mcs.st-and.ac.uk/~ns210/files/Masc-Sfak-Categor.pdf"} pdf
          %h2#operads_and_algebras Operads and algebras
          %h3#trees Trees
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} Operads
            have been used to model
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phylogenetic_tree"} phylogenetic trees
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2015/12/operads_and_phylogenetic_trees.html"} Operads and Phylogenetic Trees
          %h3#axioms_for_algebras_and_gradient_descent Axioms for algebras and gradient descent
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/little+cubes+operad"} little-disc-operad-inspired
            formalism was developed to model biological systems and cellular behaviors. This formalism considers
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gradient_descent"} gradient descent
            techniques to make certain subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{\times n}
            converge towards
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebra-like structures
          %p
            More specifically, these gradient descent techniques use the underlying equations of the axioms for algebras as
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loss_function"} objective functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            obtained through these optimizations can be interpreted as models for
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Specialization"} specialization of biological functions
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entropy"} entropic
            mechanisms in living organisms.
          %h3#references_4 References
          %p Phylogenies:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> JC Baez
              , N Otter,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Operads and Phylogenetic Trees
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/32/40/32-40abs.html"} paper
          %p Specialization and gradient descent:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Remy+Tuyeras"} R Tuyeras
              et al.,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cellular intelligence: dynamic specialization through non-equilibrium multi-scale compartmentalization
              %a{:href => "https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2021.06.25.449951v1.abstract"} bioarxiv
          %h2#related_fields Related fields
          %p This section gathers works in domains that often use category theory as a language for logical discourse.
          %h3#algebraic_topology Algebraic topology
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} Algebraic topology
            techniques and intuitions developed along with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent homology
            have been used to study neuronal morphologies.
          %p
            Additionally, the clustering algorithm
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://umap-learn.readthedocs.io/en/latest/"} UMAP
            = succeed "s." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                fuzzy simplicial set
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/fuzzy+simplicial+set"} ?
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> JC Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://johncarlosbaez.wordpress.com/2020/02/10/the-category-theory-behind-umap/"} The Category Theory Behind UMAP
          %h3#algebraic_geometry Algebraic geometry
          %p
            The concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularity"} singularities
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
          %p
            Additionally, various concepts of algebraic geometry such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifolds
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli spaces
            have been used by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Gromov"} M Gromov
            to model molecular mechanisms in living cells.
          %h3#references_5 References
          %p Persistent homology:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                L Kanari, P Dłotko, M Scolamiero, R Levi, J Shillcock,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kathryn+Hess"} K Hess
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantifying topological invariants of neuronal morphologies
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.08432"} arXiv:1603.08432
            %li
              %p
                Y Lee, SD Barthel, P Dłotko, SM Moosavi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kathryn+Hess"} K Hess
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Pore-geometry recognition: on the importance of quantifying similarity in nanoporous materials
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.06953"} arXiv:1701.06953.
          %p Fuzzy simplicial sets:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A Jackson,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The mathematics of UMAP
                %a{:href => "https://adelejackson.github.io/files/Maths_of_UMAP.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Spivak"}> DI Spivak
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Metric Realization of Fuzzy Simplicial Sets
                %a{:href => "https://math.mit.edu/~dspivak/files/metric_realization.pdf"} pdf
          %p Algebraic geometry:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                EC Zeeman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Catastrophe Theory
                %a{:href => "http://www.gaianxaos.com/pdf/dynamics/zeeman-catastrophe_theory.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Gromov"}> M Gromov
                , Mathematical slices of molecular biology,
                %a{:href => "https://www.ihes.fr/~gromov/wp-content/uploads/2018/08/M01-03.pdf"} pdf
          %h2#higher_structures Higher structures
          %h3#hyperstructures Hyperstructures
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nils+Baas"} N Baas
            has proposed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperstructure"} hyperstructures
            to describe hierarchical organizations in biology. While these structures were originally designed to organize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+cobordism"} extended cobordism
            structures, they are argued to also be appropriate for modeling multilevel systems in biology. Note that contrarily to multilevel structures such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globe"} globular arrows
            but, instead, appear to be organized as “aggregates” with
            = succeed "." do
              %em bonds
          %p
            The idea behind applying hyperstructures to biology is that they allow us to consider some set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            of “agents” such that any subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq X_0
            can define an
            %em aggregate
            when it is “labeled” by an explanation (or description)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            for that aggregation.
